/-!
# The group `Spinᶜ(Q)`

For a nonzero real quadratic form `Q`, the group `Spinᶜ(Q)` is the quotient
`(Spin(Q) × U(1)) / {1, (-1, -1)}` of the product of the Spin group with the unit circle by the
central subgroup generated by `(-1, -1)`. We write `[s, z]` for the class of `(s, z)`. It is the
structure group of a `Spinᶜ` structure: for `Q v = -‖v‖²` on an oriented four-space, its
half-spin representations `[s, z] ↦ z ρ^±(s)` are the fibres of the spinor bundles `W^±` of a
`Spinᶜ` four-manifold.

The group comes with two homomorphisms: the determinant character `[s, z] ↦ z²` to `U(1)`, and
the rotation homomorphism `[s, z] ↦ π(s)` to `SO(Q)` through the Spin action
`π : Spin(Q) → SO(Q)`. For a `Spinᶜ` structure, the determinant line `L` is the line bundle
associated to its principal `Spinᶜ` bundle through the determinant character, and the frame bundle
is obtained from that principal bundle through the rotation homomorphism. Both homomorphisms are
well defined because `(-1)² = 1` and `π(-1) = 1`. For a finite-dimensional nondegenerate `Q` in
positive dimension, the kernel of their product `Spinᶜ(Q) → SO(Q) × U(1)` is the subgroup
`{1, [1, -1]}` of order two, and the product is onto whenever the Spin action is. So `Spinᶜ(Q)` is
a double cover of `SO(Q) × U(1)` whenever `Spin(Q)` is a double cover of `SO(Q)`.

**Charge conjugation** `[s, z] ↦ [s, z⁻¹]` is an involutive automorphism of `Spinᶜ(Q)` covering
the identity of `SO(Q)` and inverting the determinant character. It is the structure-group form of
passing from a `Spinᶜ` structure to its conjugate, the `Spinᶜ` structure with the same frame
bundle, inverse determinant line and complex conjugate spinors. Since `z⁻¹ = z · (z²)⁻¹` on the
circle, it is twisting by the inverse of the determinant character,
`chargeConj x = x [1, (det x)⁻¹]`.
Consequently the conjugate of a `Spinᶜ` structure `s` with determinant line `L`, twisted by a
Hermitian line `E`, is `s` twisted by `L⁻¹ ⊗ E⁻¹`; for the canonical `Spinᶜ` structure `s_ω` of an
almost Hermitian four-manifold, whose determinant line is the anticanonical line `K⁻¹`, this is
Taubes's identification `conj (s_ω ⊗ E) ≃ s_ω ⊗ (K ⊗ E⁻¹)` at the level of structure groups.

## Main definitions

* `CliffordAlgebra.spinCKernel Q hQ`: the central subgroup of `spinGroup Q × Circle` generated by
  `(-1, -1)`.
* `CliffordAlgebra.spinCGroup Q hQ`: the quotient group `Spinᶜ(Q)`.
* `CliffordAlgebra.spinCGroup.mk Q hQ`: the quotient map `(s, z) ↦ [s, z]`, and
  `CliffordAlgebra.spinCGroup.lift`: the descent of homomorphisms trivial on `(-1, -1)`.
* `CliffordAlgebra.spinCGroup.ofSpin Q hQ` and `CliffordAlgebra.spinCGroup.ofCircle Q hQ`: the
  injective homomorphisms `s ↦ [s, 1]` and `z ↦ [1, z]`.
* `CliffordAlgebra.spinCGroup.det Q hQ`: the determinant character `[s, z] ↦ z²`.
* `CliffordAlgebra.spinCGroup.toSpecialOrthogonal Q hQ`: the rotation homomorphism
  `[s, z] ↦ π(s)`.
* `CliffordAlgebra.spinCGroup.chargeConj Q hQ`: charge conjugation `[s, z] ↦ [s, z⁻¹]`.
* `QuadraticMap.Isometry.spinCGroupMap f hQ hP`: the homomorphism `[s, z] ↦ [f s, z]` induced by
  a quadratic isometry `f`.

## Main results

* `CliffordAlgebra.mem_spinCKernel_iff`: the subgroup `spinCKernel Q hQ` is `{1, (-1, -1)}`.
* `CliffordAlgebra.coe_spinCKernel`: the underlying set of that subgroup is the diagonal pair.
* `CliffordAlgebra.finite_spinCKernel`: the diagonal kernel is finite.
* `CliffordAlgebra.spinCGroup.mk_eq_mk_iff`: `[s, z] = [s', z']` exactly when
  `(s', z') = (s, z)` or `(s', z') = (-s, -z)`.
* `CliffordAlgebra.spinCGroup.mem_ker_toSpecialOrthogonal_iff`: the kernel of the rotation
  homomorphism `Spinᶜ(Q) → SO(Q)` is the central circle `{[1, z]}`, and
  `CliffordAlgebra.spinCGroup.ker_toSpecialOrthogonal` is its subgroup form.
* `CliffordAlgebra.spinCGroup.mem_ker_toSpecialOrthogonal_prod_det_iff`: the kernel of
  `Spinᶜ(Q) → SO(Q) × U(1)` is `{1, [1, -1]}`.
* `CliffordAlgebra.spinCGroup.coe_ker_toSpecialOrthogonal_prod_det`: the underlying set of that
  kernel is the pair `{1, [1, -1]}`.
* `CliffordAlgebra.spinCGroup.finite_ker_toSpecialOrthogonal_prod_det`: that kernel is finite.
* `CliffordAlgebra.spinCGroup.toSpecialOrthogonal_prod_det_surjective`: this map is onto when the
  Spin action is onto `SO(Q)`.
* `CliffordAlgebra.spinCGroup.hom_ext_of_isSquare`: a homomorphism to `Spinᶜ(Q)` from a group in
  which every element is a square, such as the circle, is determined by its composite with
  `Spinᶜ(Q) → SO(Q) × U(1)`.
* `CliffordAlgebra.spinCGroup.det_chargeConj`,
  `CliffordAlgebra.spinCGroup.toSpecialOrthogonal_chargeConj`: charge conjugation inverts the
  determinant character and covers the identity rotation.
* `CliffordAlgebra.spinCGroup.chargeConj_eq_mul_ofCircle_inv_det`,
  `CliffordAlgebra.spinCGroup.chargeConj_mul_ofCircle`: charge conjugation is twisting by the
  inverse determinant, and the conjugate of `x` twisted by `z` is `x` twisted by `(det x)⁻¹ z⁻¹`.
* `CliffordAlgebra.spinCGroup.chargeConj_eq_self_iff`: the fixed points of charge conjugation are
  the elements with trivial determinant character.
* `QuadraticMap.Isometry.spinCGroupMap_injective`: the induced map is injective when the induced
  Spin map is.
* `QuadraticMap.Isometry.det_spinCGroupMap` and
  `QuadraticMap.Isometry.toSpecialOrthogonal_spinCGroupMap_apply`: the induced map preserves the
  determinant character, and its rotations extend the rotations of the source along `f`;
  `QuadraticMap.Isometry.spinCGroupMap_chargeConj`: it commutes with charge conjugation.

## References

* H. B. Lawson and M.-L. Michelsohn, *Spin Geometry* (1989), Appendix D.
* J. W. Morgan, *The Seiberg–Witten equations and applications to the topology of smooth
  four-manifolds*, Mathematical Notes 44, Princeton University Press, 1996, §3.1.
* C. H. Taubes, *SW ⇒ Gr: from the Seiberg–Witten equations to pseudo-holomorphic curves*,
  J. Amer. Math. Soc. 9 (1996), 845–918.
-/

public section

noncomputable section

namespace CliffordAlgebra

open TauCeti.QuadraticMap

variable {M : Type*} [AddCommGroup M] [Module ℝ M] (Q : QuadraticForm ℝ M) (hQ : Q ≠ 0)

/-- The central subgroup of `spinGroup Q × Circle` generated by `(-1, -1)`. The group
`Spinᶜ(Q)` is the quotient by it. -/
def spinCKernel : Subgroup (spinGroup Q × Circle) :=
  Subgroup.zpowers (spinGroup.negOne Q hQ, -1)

/-- The subgroup `spinCKernel Q hQ` consists of `1` and `(-1, -1)`. -/
theorem mem_spinCKernel_iff {p : spinGroup Q × Circle} :
    p ∈ spinCKernel Q hQ ↔ p = 1 ∨ p = (spinGroup.negOne Q hQ, -1) := by
  have hsq : (spinGroup.negOne Q hQ, (-1 : Circle)) ^ (2 : ℤ) = 1 := by
    ext <;> simp
  constructor
  · rintro ⟨k, rfl⟩
    dsimp only
    rw [zpow_eq_zpow_emod k hsq]
    rcases Int.emod_two_eq_zero_or_one k with h | h <;> simp [h]
  · rintro (rfl | rfl)
    · exact one_mem _
    · exact Subgroup.mem_zpowers _

/-- The underlying set of `spinCKernel Q hQ` is the diagonal pair `{1, (-1, -1)}`. -/
theorem coe_spinCKernel :
    (spinCKernel Q hQ : Set (spinGroup Q × Circle)) =
      {1, (spinGroup.negOne Q hQ, -1)} := by
  ext p
  simp [mem_spinCKernel_iff]

/-- The diagonal order-two kernel defining `Spinᶜ(Q)` is finite. -/
theorem finite_spinCKernel : (spinCKernel Q hQ : Set (spinGroup Q × Circle)).Finite := by
  rw [coe_spinCKernel]
  exact Set.toFinite _

/-- `(-1, -1)` lies in `spinCKernel Q hQ`. -/
theorem negOne_neg_one_mem_spinCKernel : (spinGroup.negOne Q hQ, -1) ∈ spinCKernel Q hQ :=
  Subgroup.mem_zpowers _

/-- The subgroup `spinCKernel Q hQ` is central. -/
theorem spinCKernel_le_center : spinCKernel Q hQ ≤ Subgroup.center (spinGroup Q × Circle) := by
  rw [spinCKernel, Subgroup.zpowers_le, Subgroup.mem_center_iff]
  intro p
  ext
  · simp
  · exact mul_comm _ _

instance : (spinCKernel Q hQ).Normal :=
  Subgroup.normal_of_le_center (spinCKernel_le_center Q hQ)

/-- **The group `Spinᶜ(Q)`**: the quotient `(Spin(Q) × U(1)) / {1, (-1, -1)}`. For
`Q v = -‖v‖²` on a real inner product space of dimension `n`, this is `Spinᶜ(n)`, the structure
group of a `Spinᶜ` structure. -/
abbrev spinCGroup : Type _ :=
  (spinGroup Q × Circle) ⧸ spinCKernel Q hQ

namespace spinCGroup

/-- The quotient map `Spin(Q) × U(1) → Spinᶜ(Q)`, `(s, z) ↦ [s, z]`. -/
def mk : spinGroup Q × Circle →* spinCGroup Q hQ :=
  QuotientGroup.mk' _

/-- `mk` is the quotient map. -/
theorem mk_apply (p : spinGroup Q × Circle) : mk Q hQ p = (p : spinCGroup Q hQ) :=
  (rfl)

/-- The underlying function of `mk` is the quotient projection. This is not a simp lemma:
simplifying partially applied `mk` would prevent representative lemmas from matching. -/
theorem coe_mk : ⇑(mk Q hQ) = QuotientGroup.mk :=
  (rfl)

/-- Every element of `Spinᶜ(Q)` is a class `[s, z]`. -/
theorem mk_surjective : Function.Surjective (mk Q hQ) :=
  QuotientGroup.mk'_surjective _

/-- The kernel of the quotient map is `{1, (-1, -1)}`. -/
@[simp]
theorem ker_mk : (mk Q hQ).ker = spinCKernel Q hQ :=
  QuotientGroup.ker_mk' _

/-- Every fibre of `Spin(Q) × Circle → Spinᶜ(Q)` has exactly two elements. -/
theorem card_fiber_mk (x : spinCGroup Q hQ) : Nat.card ((mk Q hQ) ⁻¹' {x}) = 2 := by
  rw [Nat.card_congr (MonoidHom.fiberEquivKerOfSurjective (mk_surjective Q hQ) x), ker_mk]
  refine (Nat.card_congr (Set.equivOfEq (coe_spinCKernel Q hQ))).trans ?_
  exact Set.ncard_pair (by
    intro h
    exact spinGroup.negOne_ne_one Q hQ (congrArg Prod.fst h).symm)

/-- `[-1, -1] = 1` in `Spinᶜ(Q)`. -/
@[simp]
theorem mk_negOne_neg_one : mk Q hQ (spinGroup.negOne Q hQ, -1) = 1 := by
  rw [← MonoidHom.mem_ker, ker_mk]
  exact negOne_neg_one_mem_spinCKernel Q hQ

/-- `[s, z] = [s', z']` exactly when `(s', z') = (s, z)` or `(s', z') = (-s, -z)`. -/
theorem mk_eq_mk_iff {p p' : spinGroup Q × Circle} :
    mk Q hQ p = mk Q hQ p' ↔ p' = p ∨ p' = (spinGroup.negOne Q hQ, -1) * p := by
  rw [mk_apply, mk_apply, QuotientGroup.eq, mem_spinCKernel_iff, inv_mul_eq_one,
    inv_mul_eq_iff_eq_mul, eq_comm (a := p), Subgroup.mem_center_iff.mp
      (spinCKernel_le_center Q hQ (negOne_neg_one_mem_spinCKernel Q hQ)) p]

/-- **The universal property of `Spinᶜ(Q)`**: a homomorphism out of `Spin(Q) × U(1)` that is
trivial on `(-1, -1)` descends to `Spinᶜ(Q)`. -/
def lift {G : Type*} [Monoid G] (f : spinGroup Q × Circle →* G)
    (hf : f (spinGroup.negOne Q hQ, -1) = 1) : spinCGroup Q hQ →* G :=
  QuotientGroup.lift _ f <| by
    rwa [spinCKernel, Subgroup.zpowers_le, MonoidHom.mem_ker]

@[simp]
theorem lift_mk {G : Type*} [Monoid G] (f : spinGroup Q × Circle →* G)
    (hf : f (spinGroup.negOne Q hQ, -1) = 1) (p : spinGroup Q × Circle) :
    lift Q hQ f hf (mk Q hQ p) = f p :=
  (rfl)

/-- The composite of `lift f` with the quotient map is `f`. -/
@[simp]
theorem lift_comp_mk {G : Type*} [Monoid G] (f : spinGroup Q × Circle →* G)
    (hf : f (spinGroup.negOne Q hQ, -1) = 1) : (lift Q hQ f hf).comp (mk Q hQ) = f :=
  (rfl)

/-- The inclusion `Spin(Q) → Spinᶜ(Q)`, `s ↦ [s, 1]`. -/
def ofSpin : spinGroup Q →* spinCGroup Q hQ :=
  (mk Q hQ).comp (MonoidHom.inl _ _)

/-- The inclusion `U(1) → Spinᶜ(Q)`, `z ↦ [1, z]`. -/
def ofCircle : Circle →* spinCGroup Q hQ :=
  (mk Q hQ).comp (MonoidHom.inr _ _)

theorem ofSpin_apply (s : spinGroup Q) : ofSpin Q hQ s = mk Q hQ (s, 1) :=
  (rfl)

theorem ofCircle_apply (z : Circle) : ofCircle Q hQ z = mk Q hQ (1, z) :=
  (rfl)

/-- The class `[s, z]` is the product `[s, 1] [1, z]`. -/
theorem ofSpin_mul_ofCircle (s : spinGroup Q) (z : Circle) :
    ofSpin Q hQ s * ofCircle Q hQ z = mk Q hQ (s, z) := by
  simp [ofSpin_apply, ofCircle_apply, ← map_mul]

/-- The circle factor is central in `Spinᶜ(Q)`. -/
theorem ofCircle_mem_center (z : Circle) :
    ofCircle Q hQ z ∈ Subgroup.center (spinCGroup Q hQ) := by
  rw [Subgroup.mem_center_iff]
  intro x
  obtain ⟨⟨s, w⟩, rfl⟩ := mk_surjective Q hQ x
  simp only [ofCircle_apply, ← map_mul, Prod.mk_mul_mk, mul_one, one_mul, mul_comm z]

/-- The two inclusions agree on `-1`: `[-1, 1] = [1, -1]`. -/
theorem ofSpin_negOne : ofSpin Q hQ (spinGroup.negOne Q hQ) = ofCircle Q hQ (-1) := by
  rw [ofSpin_apply, ofCircle_apply, mk_eq_mk_iff]
  right
  ext <;> simp

/-- `Spin(Q)` is a subgroup of `Spinᶜ(Q)`. -/
theorem ofSpin_injective : Function.Injective (ofSpin Q hQ) := by
  intro s s' h
  rcases (mk_eq_mk_iff Q hQ).mp h with h | h
  · exact (Prod.ext_iff.mp h).1.symm
  · exact absurd (Prod.ext_iff.mp h).2 (by simpa using (Circle.neg_ne_self (1 : Circle)).symm)

/-- `U(1)` is a subgroup of `Spinᶜ(Q)`. -/
theorem ofCircle_injective : Function.Injective (ofCircle Q hQ) := by
  intro z z' h
  rcases (mk_eq_mk_iff Q hQ).mp h with h | h
  · exact (Prod.ext_iff.mp h).2.symm
  · exact absurd (by simpa using (Prod.ext_iff.mp h).1.symm) (spinGroup.negOne_ne_one Q hQ)

/-! ### The determinant character -/

/-- **The determinant character** `Spinᶜ(Q) → U(1)`, `[s, z] ↦ z²`, well defined because
`(-1)² = 1`. The determinant line of a `Spinᶜ` structure is the line bundle associated to its
principal `Spinᶜ` bundle through this character. -/
def det : spinCGroup Q hQ →* Circle :=
  lift Q hQ ((powMonoidHom 2).comp (MonoidHom.snd _ _)) (by simp)

@[simp]
theorem det_mk (p : spinGroup Q × Circle) : det Q hQ (mk Q hQ p) = p.2 ^ 2 :=
  (rfl)

@[simp]
theorem det_ofSpin (s : spinGroup Q) : det Q hQ (ofSpin Q hQ s) = 1 := by
  simp [ofSpin_apply]

@[simp]
theorem det_ofCircle (z : Circle) : det Q hQ (ofCircle Q hQ z) = z ^ 2 := by
  simp [ofCircle_apply]

/-! ### The rotation homomorphism -/

/-- **The rotation homomorphism** `Spinᶜ(Q) → SO(Q)`, `[s, z] ↦ π(s)`, through the Spin action
`π`; it is well defined because `π(-1) = 1`. -/
def toSpecialOrthogonal : spinCGroup Q hQ →* specialOrthogonalGroup Q :=
  lift Q hQ ((spinToSpecialOrthogonal Q).comp (MonoidHom.fst _ _)) (by simp)

@[simp]
theorem toSpecialOrthogonal_mk (p : spinGroup Q × Circle) :
    toSpecialOrthogonal Q hQ (mk Q hQ p) = spinToSpecialOrthogonal Q p.1 :=
  (rfl)

@[simp]
theorem toSpecialOrthogonal_ofSpin (s : spinGroup Q) :
    toSpecialOrthogonal Q hQ (ofSpin Q hQ s) = spinToSpecialOrthogonal Q s := by
  simp [ofSpin_apply]

@[simp]
theorem toSpecialOrthogonal_ofCircle (z : Circle) :
    toSpecialOrthogonal Q hQ (ofCircle Q hQ z) = 1 := by
  simp [ofCircle_apply]

/-- **The kernel of the rotation homomorphism `Spinᶜ(Q) → SO(Q)` is the central circle**
`{[1, z]}`, for a finite-dimensional nondegenerate quadratic form. -/
theorem mem_ker_toSpecialOrthogonal_iff [FiniteDimensional ℝ M] (hQn : Q.Nondegenerate)
    (x : spinCGroup Q hQ) :
    x ∈ (toSpecialOrthogonal Q hQ).ker ↔ ∃ z : Circle, x = ofCircle Q hQ z := by
  have : Nontrivial M := QuadraticMap.nontrivial_of_ne_zero hQ
  constructor
  · intro hx
    obtain ⟨⟨s, z⟩, rfl⟩ := mk_surjective Q hQ x
    rw [MonoidHom.mem_ker, toSpecialOrthogonal_mk] at hx
    rcases (mem_ker_spinToSpecialOrthogonal_iff Q hQn s).mp hx with rfl | rfl
    · exact ⟨z, (ofCircle_apply Q hQ z).symm⟩
    · refine ⟨-z, ?_⟩
      rw [← ofSpin_mul_ofCircle, ofSpin_negOne, ← map_mul, neg_one_mul]
  · rintro ⟨z, rfl⟩
    exact toSpecialOrthogonal_ofCircle Q hQ z

/-- The kernel of the rotation homomorphism `Spinᶜ(Q) → SO(Q)` is the range of `ofCircle`, for a
finite-dimensional nondegenerate quadratic form. -/
theorem ker_toSpecialOrthogonal [FiniteDimensional ℝ M] (hQn : Q.Nondegenerate) :
    (toSpecialOrthogonal Q hQ).ker = (ofCircle Q hQ).range :=
  Subgroup.ext fun x ↦ (mem_ker_toSpecialOrthogonal_iff Q hQ hQn x).trans <|
    exists_congr fun _ ↦ eq_comm

/-! ### `Spinᶜ(Q)` as a double cover of `SO(Q) × U(1)` -/

/-- **The kernel of `Spinᶜ(Q) → SO(Q) × U(1)`**, `[s, z] ↦ (π(s), z²)`, is `{1, [1, -1]}` for a
finite-dimensional nondegenerate quadratic form in positive dimension. -/
theorem mem_ker_toSpecialOrthogonal_prod_det_iff [FiniteDimensional ℝ M]
    (hQn : Q.Nondegenerate) (x : spinCGroup Q hQ) :
    x ∈ ((toSpecialOrthogonal Q hQ).prod (det Q hQ)).ker ↔ x = 1 ∨ x = ofCircle Q hQ (-1) := by
  have : Nontrivial M := QuadraticMap.nontrivial_of_ne_zero hQ
  obtain ⟨⟨s, z⟩, rfl⟩ := mk_surjective Q hQ x
  constructor
  · intro hx
    rw [MonoidHom.mem_ker, MonoidHom.prod_apply, Prod.mk_eq_one, toSpecialOrthogonal_mk,
      det_mk] at hx
    have hz : z = 1 ∨ z = -1 := by
      have h := congrArg ((↑) : Circle → ℂ) hx.2
      rw [Circle.coe_pow, Circle.coe_one, sq, mul_self_eq_one_iff] at h
      exact h.imp (fun h ↦ Circle.ext (by simpa using h)) (fun h ↦ Circle.ext (by simpa using h))
    rcases (mem_ker_spinToSpecialOrthogonal_iff Q hQn s).mp hx.1 with rfl | rfl <;>
      rcases hz with rfl | rfl
    · exact Or.inl (map_one _)
    · exact Or.inr rfl
    · refine Or.inr ((mk_eq_mk_iff Q hQ).mpr (Or.inr ?_))
      ext <;> simp
    · exact Or.inl (mk_negOne_neg_one Q hQ)
  · rintro (h | h) <;> simp [h]

/-- The underlying set of the frame-and-determinant kernel is `{1, [1, -1]}` for a
finite-dimensional nondegenerate form in positive dimension. -/
theorem coe_ker_toSpecialOrthogonal_prod_det [FiniteDimensional ℝ M]
    (hQn : Q.Nondegenerate) :
    (((toSpecialOrthogonal Q hQ).prod (det Q hQ)).ker : Set (spinCGroup Q hQ)) =
      {1, ofCircle Q hQ (-1)} := by
  ext x
  simp [mem_ker_toSpecialOrthogonal_prod_det_iff Q hQ hQn]

/-- The frame-and-determinant kernel is finite for a nondegenerate form in positive finite
dimension. -/
theorem finite_ker_toSpecialOrthogonal_prod_det [FiniteDimensional ℝ M]
    (hQn : Q.Nondegenerate) :
    (((toSpecialOrthogonal Q hQ).prod (det Q hQ)).ker : Set (spinCGroup Q hQ)).Finite := by
  rw [coe_ker_toSpecialOrthogonal_prod_det Q hQ hQn]
  exact Set.toFinite _

/-- **`Spinᶜ(Q) → SO(Q) × U(1)` is onto** whenever the Spin action is onto `SO(Q)`: every unit
complex number has a square root on the circle. -/
theorem toSpecialOrthogonal_prod_det_surjective
    (h : Function.Surjective (spinToSpecialOrthogonal Q)) :
    Function.Surjective ((toSpecialOrthogonal Q hQ).prod (det Q hQ)) := by
  rintro ⟨g, z⟩
  obtain ⟨s, rfl⟩ := h g
  refine ⟨mk Q hQ (s, Circle.exp (Complex.arg z / 2)), ?_⟩
  rw [MonoidHom.prod_apply, toSpecialOrthogonal_mk, det_mk, ← Circle.exp_nsmul, nsmul_eq_mul,
    Nat.cast_ofNat, mul_div_cancel₀ _ two_ne_zero, Circle.exp_arg]

/-- Every fibre of the frame-and-determinant map has two elements when it is surjective and
the form is nondegenerate in positive finite dimension. -/
theorem card_fiber_toSpecialOrthogonal_prod_det [FiniteDimensional ℝ M]
    (hQn : Q.Nondegenerate)
    (hsurj : Function.Surjective ((toSpecialOrthogonal Q hQ).prod (det Q hQ)))
    (y : TauCeti.QuadraticMap.specialOrthogonalGroup Q × Circle) :
    Nat.card (((toSpecialOrthogonal Q hQ).prod (det Q hQ)) ⁻¹' {y}) = 2 := by
  rw [Nat.card_congr (MonoidHom.fiberEquivKerOfSurjective hsurj y)]
  refine (Nat.card_congr
    (Set.equivOfEq (coe_ker_toSpecialOrthogonal_prod_det Q hQ hQn))).trans ?_
  exact Set.ncard_pair (by
    intro h
    have hone : ofCircle Q hQ (1 : Circle) = 1 := map_one _
    exact Circle.neg_ne_self (1 : Circle)
      ((ofCircle_injective Q hQ) (h.symm.trans hone.symm)))

/-! ### Homomorphisms from monoids of squares -/

/-- **A homomorphism to `Spinᶜ(Q)` from a monoid in which every element is a square is determined
by its composite with `Spinᶜ(Q) → SO(Q) × U(1)`**, for a finite-dimensional nondegenerate form.
Two such homomorphisms differ by a homomorphism into the central kernel `{1, [1, -1]}`, and this is
trivial because every element of the source is a square while the kernel has exponent two. This
applies to the circle (`Circle.isSquare`) and to the unitary groups
(`Matrix.UnitaryGroup.isSquare`). -/
theorem hom_ext_of_isSquare [FiniteDimensional ℝ M] (hQn : Q.Nondegenerate) {G : Type*} [Monoid G]
    (hG : ∀ g : G, IsSquare g) {φ ψ : G →* spinCGroup Q hQ}
    (h : ((toSpecialOrthogonal Q hQ).prod (det Q hQ)).comp φ =
      ((toSpecialOrthogonal Q hQ).prod (det Q hQ)).comp ψ) :
    φ = ψ := by
  have : Nontrivial M := QuadraticMap.nontrivial_of_ne_zero hQ
  -- The defect `φ y (ψ y)⁻¹` lies in the kernel `{1, [1, -1]}`: it is central and squares to `1`.
  have hk (y : G) :
      φ y * (ψ y)⁻¹ ∈ Subgroup.center (spinCGroup Q hQ) ∧ (φ y * (ψ y)⁻¹) ^ 2 = 1 := by
    have hmem : φ y * (ψ y)⁻¹ ∈ ((toSpecialOrthogonal Q hQ).prod (det Q hQ)).ker := by
      rw [MonoidHom.mem_ker, map_mul, map_inv, ← MonoidHom.comp_apply, h, MonoidHom.comp_apply,
        mul_inv_cancel]
    rcases (mem_ker_toSpecialOrthogonal_prod_det_iff Q hQ hQn _).mp hmem with h₁ | h₁ <;>
      rw [h₁]
    · exact ⟨one_mem _, one_pow 2⟩
    · exact ⟨ofCircle_mem_center Q hQ _, by rw [← map_pow]; simp⟩
  ext z
  obtain ⟨y, rfl⟩ := hG z
  obtain ⟨hc, hsq⟩ := hk y
  have hcomm : Commute (φ y * (ψ y)⁻¹) (ψ y) := (Subgroup.mem_center_iff.mp hc (ψ y)).symm
  rw [← sq, map_pow, map_pow, ← inv_mul_cancel_right (φ y) (ψ y), hcomm.mul_pow, hsq, one_mul]

/-! ### Charge conjugation -/

/-- **Charge conjugation** of `Spinᶜ(Q)`, the involutive automorphism `[s, z] ↦ [s, z⁻¹]`,
well defined because `(-1)⁻¹ = -1`. It fixes `Spin(Q)`, so it covers the identity of `SO(Q)`, and
it inverts the determinant character. A principal `Spinᶜ` bundle with transition functions
`g_αβ` therefore has a conjugate with transition functions `chargeConj g_αβ`: its frame bundle is
the same and its determinant line is the inverse. This is the conjugate `Spinᶜ` structure, whose
spinors are the complex conjugates of the original ones
(`CliffordAlgebra.map_coe_spinCGroupHalfSpinRep_apply`). -/
def chargeConj : spinCGroup Q hQ ≃* spinCGroup Q hQ where
  toFun := lift Q hQ ((mk Q hQ).comp ((MonoidHom.id _).prodMap invMonoidHom)) (by simp)
  invFun := lift Q hQ ((mk Q hQ).comp ((MonoidHom.id _).prodMap invMonoidHom)) (by simp)
  left_inv x := by
    obtain ⟨⟨s, z⟩, rfl⟩ := mk_surjective Q hQ x
    simp
  right_inv x := by
    obtain ⟨⟨s, z⟩, rfl⟩ := mk_surjective Q hQ x
    simp
  map_mul' := map_mul _

/-- Charge conjugation sends `[s, z]` to `[s, z⁻¹]`. -/
@[simp]
theorem chargeConj_mk (p : spinGroup Q × Circle) :
    chargeConj Q hQ (mk Q hQ p) = mk Q hQ (p.1, p.2⁻¹) :=
  (rfl)

/-- Charge conjugation is its own inverse. -/
@[simp]
theorem chargeConj_symm : (chargeConj Q hQ).symm = chargeConj Q hQ :=
  (rfl)

/-- Charge conjugation is an involution. -/
@[simp]
theorem chargeConj_chargeConj (x : spinCGroup Q hQ) :
    chargeConj Q hQ (chargeConj Q hQ x) = x :=
  (chargeConj Q hQ).symm_apply_apply x

/-- Charge conjugation fixes `Spin(Q) ⊆ Spinᶜ(Q)`. -/
@[simp]
theorem chargeConj_ofSpin (s : spinGroup Q) :
    chargeConj Q hQ (ofSpin Q hQ s) = ofSpin Q hQ s := by
  simp [ofSpin_apply]

/-- Charge conjugation inverts the central circle `U(1) ⊆ Spinᶜ(Q)`. -/
@[simp]
theorem chargeConj_ofCircle (z : Circle) :
    chargeConj Q hQ (ofCircle Q hQ z) = ofCircle Q hQ z⁻¹ := by
  simp [ofCircle_apply]

/-- **Charge conjugation inverts the determinant character**: the determinant line of the
conjugate `Spinᶜ` structure is the inverse of the original one. -/
@[simp]
theorem det_chargeConj (x : spinCGroup Q hQ) :
    det Q hQ (chargeConj Q hQ x) = (det Q hQ x)⁻¹ := by
  obtain ⟨p, rfl⟩ := mk_surjective Q hQ x
  simp

/-- **Charge conjugation covers the identity rotation**: the conjugate `Spinᶜ` structure has the
same frame bundle. -/
@[simp]
theorem toSpecialOrthogonal_chargeConj (x : spinCGroup Q hQ) :
    toSpecialOrthogonal Q hQ (chargeConj Q hQ x) = toSpecialOrthogonal Q hQ x := by
  obtain ⟨p, rfl⟩ := mk_surjective Q hQ x
  simp

/-- **Charge conjugation is twisting by the inverse determinant**:
`chargeConj x = x [1, (det x)⁻¹]`. Since `[s, z⁻¹] = [s, z] [1, (z²)⁻¹]`, the conjugate of a
`Spinᶜ` structure `s` with determinant line `L` is `s` twisted by the line `L⁻¹`. -/
theorem chargeConj_eq_mul_ofCircle_inv_det (x : spinCGroup Q hQ) :
    chargeConj Q hQ x = x * ofCircle Q hQ (det Q hQ x)⁻¹ := by
  obtain ⟨⟨s, z⟩, rfl⟩ := mk_surjective Q hQ x
  simp [ofCircle_apply, ← map_mul, pow_two]

/-- **The conjugate of a twisted `Spinᶜ` structure**:
`chargeConj (x [1, z]) = x [1, (det x)⁻¹ z⁻¹]`.
Twisting by `[1, z]` is tensoring the spinors with a Hermitian line `E`, so the conjugate of
`s ⊗ E` is `s ⊗ (L⁻¹ ⊗ E⁻¹)`, where `L` is the determinant line of `s`. For the canonical `Spinᶜ`
structure of an almost Hermitian four-manifold, `L = K⁻¹` and this is the isomorphism
`conj (s_ω ⊗ E) ≃ s_ω ⊗ (K ⊗ E⁻¹)` at the level of structure groups. -/
theorem chargeConj_mul_ofCircle (x : spinCGroup Q hQ) (z : Circle) :
    chargeConj Q hQ (x * ofCircle Q hQ z) = x * ofCircle Q hQ ((det Q hQ x)⁻¹ * z⁻¹) := by
  rw [map_mul, chargeConj_ofCircle, chargeConj_eq_mul_ofCircle_inv_det, map_mul, mul_assoc]

/-- An element is fixed by charge conjugation exactly when its determinant character is trivial. -/
theorem chargeConj_eq_self_iff {x : spinCGroup Q hQ} :
    chargeConj Q hQ x = x ↔ det Q hQ x = 1 := by
  rw [chargeConj_eq_mul_ofCircle_inv_det]
  simp [map_eq_one_iff _ (ofCircle_injective Q hQ)]

end spinCGroup

end CliffordAlgebra

/-! ### Functoriality -/

namespace QuadraticMap.Isometry

open CliffordAlgebra

variable {M N L : Type*} [AddCommGroup M] [Module ℝ M] [AddCommGroup N] [Module ℝ N]
  [AddCommGroup L] [Module ℝ L] {Q : QuadraticForm ℝ M} {P : QuadraticForm ℝ N}
  {R : QuadraticForm ℝ L} (f : Q →qᵢ P) (hQ : Q ≠ 0) (hP : P ≠ 0)

/-- **The homomorphism of `Spinᶜ` groups induced by a quadratic isometry** `f`,
`[s, z] ↦ [f s, z]`, where `f s` is the image of `s` under the induced homomorphism
`QuadraticMap.Isometry.spinGroupMap` of Spin groups. It is well defined because `f` preserves the
scalar `-1`. For the inclusion of an orthogonal summand, such as the stabilization `V → ℝ × V` by
an extra line, it extends the structure group of a `Spinᶜ` structure on `V` to that of `ℝ × V`. -/
def spinCGroupMap : spinCGroup Q hQ →* spinCGroup P hP :=
  spinCGroup.lift Q hQ ((spinCGroup.mk P hP).comp (f.spinGroupMap.prodMap (MonoidHom.id Circle)))
    (by simp)

/-- The induced map sends `[s, z]` to `[f s, z]`. -/
@[simp]
theorem spinCGroupMap_mk (p : spinGroup Q × Circle) :
    f.spinCGroupMap hQ hP (spinCGroup.mk Q hQ p) = spinCGroup.mk P hP (f.spinGroupMap p.1, p.2) :=
  (rfl)

@[simp]
theorem spinCGroupMap_ofSpin (s : spinGroup Q) :
    f.spinCGroupMap hQ hP (spinCGroup.ofSpin Q hQ s) =
      spinCGroup.ofSpin P hP (f.spinGroupMap s) := by
  simp [spinCGroup.ofSpin_apply]

@[simp]
theorem spinCGroupMap_ofCircle (z : Circle) :
    f.spinCGroupMap hQ hP (spinCGroup.ofCircle Q hQ z) = spinCGroup.ofCircle P hP z := by
  simp [spinCGroup.ofCircle_apply]

/-- The identity isometry induces the identity of `Spinᶜ(Q)`. -/
@[simp]
theorem spinCGroupMap_id : (Isometry.id Q).spinCGroupMap hQ hQ = MonoidHom.id _ := by
  refine MonoidHom.ext fun x ↦ ?_
  obtain ⟨p, rfl⟩ := spinCGroup.mk_surjective Q hQ x
  simp

/-- The maps induced by quadratic isometries respect composition. -/
@[simp]
theorem spinCGroupMap_comp (g : P →qᵢ R) (hR : R ≠ 0) :
    (g.spinCGroupMap hP hR).comp (f.spinCGroupMap hQ hP) = (g.comp f).spinCGroupMap hQ hR := by
  refine MonoidHom.ext fun x ↦ ?_
  obtain ⟨p, rfl⟩ := spinCGroup.mk_surjective Q hQ x
  rw [MonoidHom.comp_apply, spinCGroupMap_mk, spinCGroupMap_mk, spinCGroupMap_mk,
    ← spinGroupMap_comp g f, MonoidHom.comp_apply]

/-- **The induced map is injective when the induced Spin map is**: it sends `[s, z]` to
`[f s, z]`, and `f` preserves the scalar `-1`. -/
theorem spinCGroupMap_injective (hf : Function.Injective f.spinGroupMap) :
    Function.Injective (f.spinCGroupMap hQ hP) := by
  rw [injective_iff_map_eq_one]
  intro x hx
  obtain ⟨⟨s, z⟩, rfl⟩ := spinCGroup.mk_surjective Q hQ x
  rw [spinCGroupMap_mk, ← map_one (spinCGroup.mk P hP), spinCGroup.mk_eq_mk_iff] at hx
  rw [← map_one (spinCGroup.mk Q hQ), spinCGroup.mk_eq_mk_iff]
  rcases hx with h | h
  · left
    simp only [Prod.ext_iff, Prod.fst_one, Prod.snd_one] at h ⊢
    exact ⟨hf (by rw [map_one, h.1]), h.2⟩
  · right
    simp only [Prod.ext_iff, Prod.fst_mul, Prod.snd_mul, Prod.fst_one, Prod.snd_one] at h ⊢
    refine ⟨hf ?_, h.2⟩
    rw [map_one, h.1, map_mul, spinGroupMap_negOne]

/-- The induced map preserves the determinant character. -/
@[simp]
theorem det_spinCGroupMap (x : spinCGroup Q hQ) :
    spinCGroup.det P hP (f.spinCGroupMap hQ hP x) = spinCGroup.det Q hQ x := by
  obtain ⟨p, rfl⟩ := spinCGroup.mk_surjective Q hQ x
  simp

/-- **The rotation of an induced element extends the rotation of the source along `f`**: on the
image of `f`, the rotation `π([f s, z])` acts as `f ∘ π([s, z])`. -/
@[simp]
theorem toSpecialOrthogonal_spinCGroupMap_apply (x : spinCGroup Q hQ) (m : M) :
    (spinCGroup.toSpecialOrthogonal P hP (f.spinCGroupMap hQ hP x) : N ≃ₗ[ℝ] N) (f m) =
      f ((spinCGroup.toSpecialOrthogonal Q hQ x : M ≃ₗ[ℝ] M) m) := by
  obtain ⟨p, rfl⟩ := spinCGroup.mk_surjective Q hQ x
  simp

/-- The induced map commutes with charge conjugation. -/
@[simp]
theorem spinCGroupMap_chargeConj (x : spinCGroup Q hQ) :
    f.spinCGroupMap hQ hP (spinCGroup.chargeConj Q hQ x) =
      spinCGroup.chargeConj P hP (f.spinCGroupMap hQ hP x) := by
  obtain ⟨p, rfl⟩ := spinCGroup.mk_surjective Q hQ x
  simp

end QuadraticMap.Isometry
